  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Potential strong storms this afternoon/evening\nand again Friday. Damaging winds and localized flooding\npossible.\n\nAn upper trough will remain in place over much of the eastern CONUS\ntoday. Moderate destabilization will be possible along/ahead of a\nweak surface trough/front extending from central NY/PA into the\ncentral/southern Appalachians. Scattered thunderstorm development is\nexpected by mid/late afternoon.\n\nDeep-layer shear will be weaker across our are but with a \nrelatively warm and well- mixed environment will support \npotential for localized strong/damaging gusts from any storms.\n\nPer convective allowing models appears coverage today will be\nmore scattered with best coverage along the southern Blue Ridge.\n\nElevated PWATs will also suffice in having torrential downpours.\nStorm motions will be slow in the 15 to 20 mph range, but very\nwill could see some anchoring along the mountains with low \nlevel weak south flow and flow aloft from the west-southwest.\n\nStorms may linger well past midnight but weaker and still fairly\nisolated.\n\nFriday, the front slows and stalls somewhere across the VA/NC\nPiedmont/Foothills, as several vorts track from the TN Valley to\nthe east coast. Looks like better severe threat appears to stay\neast of the mountains, with more instability but will depend on\ncloud coverage early in the day. If sunshine is plentiful expect\nmoderate to potentially strong instability east/southeast of the\nfront where upper 60s to low 70s dewpoints will be present. \nLapse rates will be quite weak, but most guidance shows \ntemperatures warming into the low to mid 80s which should \nprovide pockets of moderate to strong instability. Any storms \nthat can take advantage of the a stronger flow aloft may be \nlonger- lived and capable of some damaging wind gusts. \n\nAgain with stalled front, potential for training along and east\nof the front may cause localized flooding issues. Again this\nwill be more east of the Blue Ridge.\n\nGiven the scattered nature in the convection am not seeing any\nneed to post any flood watches.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Heat builds into next week.\n\nUnder the influence of a main upper high/ridge, high temperatures\nwill be above normal by early next week with highs in the 90s\nagain in the Piedmont and mid to upper 80s in the mountains.\nStorm chances will be limited due to the warming aloft.",
